slide layout=cover align=center valign=middle justify=#false {
  title "Presenting and shipping"
  text "From the first keypress to a published site."
  text "Controls, presenter view, export, deployment · press **→**"
  notes #"""
    Presenting and shipping: part of the slides manual. Source: examples/presenting.
    """#
}

slide --text-size="4vmin" {
  heading "What's inside"
  list numbered {
    - "Controls and the slide drawer"
    - "The presenter window and tools"
    - "PDF, static site, single file, executable"
    - "GitHub Pages"
    - "Writing decks with an LLM"
  }
  notes #"""
    Press o at any time for the slide drawer, h for help. Every slide in this deck is live: it uses the feature it describes.
    """#
}

slide --text-size="3.8vmin" {
  heading "Controls"
  list {
    - "**→ Space PageDown Enter** next step · **← PageUp Backspace** previous"
    - "**Home / End** first / last · click right 3/4 \\= next, left 1/4 \\= back · swipe on touch screens"
    - "**o** slide drawer (thumbnails of all slides, stays open while you present) · **t** theme · **f** fullscreen · **p** presenter window · **h** help"
    - "URL: `#12` jumps to step 12, `?theme=paper` picks a theme, `?deck=name` picks a deck"
  }
  notes #"""
    Steps are numbered across the whole deck; every animation frame is a step.
    """#
}

slide {
  heading "Presenter window, notes and tools"
  text "Press **p**: a second window shows your notes (the HTML comments), the next slide and a timer. It stays in sync with the audience window, both ways — you can drive from either."
  text "On the slide itself: **l** a laser pointer, **d** draw (**c** clears, **Esc** stops), **z** a 2× magnifier, **a** autoplay (or `\"autoplay\": 5` in `deck.json`, `?autoplay=5` in the URL). A slide with `hidden: true` in its `<slide>` block is skipped in the show and the PDF."
  code "typescript" {
    frame #"""
      // the presenter view lists the HTML comments of the current slide
      const notes = slide.notes;
      """#
  }
  notes #"""
    Like this one. Every HTML comment in a slide becomes one paragraph of notes.
    """#
}

slide --text-size="3.8vmin" {
  heading "Slide drawer and export"
  list {
    - "**o** (or the ☰ button that appears when you move the mouse) opens the drawer: a thumbnail of every slide, the current one highlighted. Click to jump; **Esc** or **o** closes it"
    - "`?print=slides` — one page per slide, `?print=steps` — one page per animation step; then *Print → Save as PDF*"
    - "or from the command line: `slideit build deck/ -t pdf [--steps]`"
  }
}

slide {
  heading "Build targets"
  code "bash" {
    frame #"""
      slides dev   my-talk/                       # dev server, live reload
      slides build my-talk/ -o dist               # static website
      slides build my-talk/ -t single -o talk.html  # one file, works offline
      slides build my-talk/ -t pdf  -o talk.pdf   # PDF (Chromium needed)
      slides build my-talk/ -t exe  -o talk       # standalone executable
      """#
  }
  notes #"""
    static: any hosting, --base for sub-paths. single: everything inline as data URIs. pdf: --steps for one page per step. exe: Node single-executable app for the current platform.
    """#
}

slide {
  heading "Publish on GitHub Pages"
  text "The slideit action checks the deck, builds the site (and a PDF) and uploads it for Pages:"
  code "yaml" {
    frame #"""
      jobs:
        build:
          runs-on: ubuntu-latest
          steps:
            - uses: actions/checkout@v4
            - uses: geraldserafin/md-slides@action-v1
              with:
                deck: talk
                pdf: 'true'
        deploy:
          needs: build
          runs-on: ubuntu-latest
          environment: github-pages
          steps:
            - uses: actions/deploy-pages@v4
      """#
  }
  text "Or press **Use this template** on `geraldserafin/slideit-template`: a deck and this workflow, ready to push."
  notes #"""
    Settings, Pages, Source: GitHub Actions, once. The action's inputs: deck, output, pdf, check, pages, hub, version.
    """#
}

slide {
  heading "Write decks with an LLM"
  text "The site serves `llms.txt`, a compact syntax reference written for language models, and `llms-full.txt`, this manual in one file. Give a model the URL, describe your talk, and ask for a deck folder:"
  code "text" {
    frame #"""
      Read https://geraldserafin.github.io/md-slides/llms.txt,
      then write me a deck about how Git branches work.
      """#
  }
  text "Review it with `slideit dev`, adjust, and ship it."
  notes #"""
    One .slide file per slide plus a deck.json: easy for a model to produce and easy to diff when you edit its draft.
    """#
}

slide --text-size="3.8vmin" {
  heading "Tips"
  list {
    - "keep frames **small and incremental**: one idea per step gives the cleanest motion"
    - "copy the previous frame and edit it; unrelated reformatting looks like noise"
    - "the animation needs the *same language* in consecutive blocks; a language change cross-fades instead"
    - "use `size=` when you want exact font sizes, `.left`/`.right` to align code beside text"
    - "every feature is plain files: put decks in git, build them in CI"
  }
  notes #"""
    Add matcher regression cases to tests/fixtures when a transition looks wrong.
    """#
}

slide theme=midnight layout=cover align=center valign=middle justify=#false background="bg.svg" transition=zoom {
  title "That's all"
  text "More decks in this series: **Showcase**, **Writing slides**, **Code animation**, **Theming and layout**."
  text "Start your own: `slideit dev my-talk/`"
  notes #"""
    The end.
    """#
}
